00:34:26.790 --> 00:34:31.590
He's so well aware what,
how he situated himself,

00:34:31.590 --> 00:34:34.326
like, "I can't be too welcoming.

00:34:34.326 --> 00:34:37.440
I can also be realistic about this."

00:34:37.440 --> 00:34:39.540
Is there a particular side or something

00:34:39.540 --> 00:34:40.740
that you wanted to show?

00:34:41.753 --> 00:34:44.608
- Yeah, the idea, first
of all, for example,

00:34:44.608 --> 00:34:49.608
we decided to not alter the
titles, the country of origin

00:34:50.011 --> 00:34:53.700
because we didn't want
to put another label,

00:34:53.700 --> 00:34:56.220
we didn't do for all the
other people interviewed.

00:34:56.220 --> 00:34:59.322
We didn't put Italy, Malta
and we didn't want do that

00:34:59.322 --> 00:35:02.070
with the refugees.

00:35:02.070 --> 00:35:05.550
So, this was a specific choice
that we didn't do very simply

00:35:05.550 --> 00:35:08.130
in the other two previous documentaries.

00:35:08.130 --> 00:35:11.040
The third part of the trilogy that we,

00:35:11.040 --> 00:35:12.963
the other documentaries, we decided.

00:35:14.040 --> 00:35:19.040
And the idea was to, we tried,

00:35:19.710 --> 00:35:24.710
we strongly tried to give an
image of the refugees as less

00:35:26.250 --> 00:35:27.693
emotional as we could.

00:35:28.740 --> 00:35:31.350
Usually in this kind of contents,

00:35:31.350 --> 00:35:33.780
the image of the refugees that we see

00:35:33.780 --> 00:35:37.890
inevitably also because of the
context are very emotional,

00:35:37.890 --> 00:35:39.807
are images that you,

00:35:39.807 --> 00:35:42.480
you see a documentary and
sometimes you can't help

00:35:42.480 --> 00:35:46.590
but to cry because their
stories and their conditions

00:35:46.590 --> 00:35:48.780
are difficult.

00:35:48.780 --> 00:35:52.110
We wanted to completely
change this narrative

00:35:52.110 --> 00:35:53.280
in this documentary,

00:35:53.280 --> 00:35:55.590
not because we wanted to
deny their [Indistinct]

00:35:55.590 --> 00:35:57.990
but because we wanted to
show something completely

00:35:57.990 --> 00:35:58.950
different.

00:35:58.950 --> 00:36:01.579
That's why we, for
example, you mentioned it,

00:36:01.579 --> 00:36:04.830
we put working student, not just refugee

00:36:04.830 --> 00:36:08.130
because we wanted to make clear that these

00:36:08.130 --> 00:36:12.810
are human beings that are
trying to live in this country

00:36:12.810 --> 00:36:16.770
to study and to work at a
cost of enormous sacrifices

00:36:16.770 --> 00:36:18.720
because it's not easy at all.

00:36:18.720 --> 00:36:23.720
So the idea was to humanize
them as much as possible,

00:36:26.400 --> 00:36:29.550
trying to put them out
of this categorization

00:36:29.550 --> 00:36:31.860
that inevitably we put them in

00:36:31.860 --> 00:36:34.860
when we look at them in
context like refugee camps

00:36:34.860 --> 00:36:38.739
or when they just arrived
after three days trip

00:36:38.739 --> 00:36:40.530
in the Mediterranean.

00:36:40.530 --> 00:36:45.377
But to give to the audience
the perception that

00:36:45.377 --> 00:36:49.670
these are not Maltese.

00:36:49.670 --> 00:36:54.450
In 10 years, awfully these
are Maltese working, living.

00:36:54.450 --> 00:36:59.450
And that's also why we wanted
to close the documentary

00:37:00.030 --> 00:37:05.030
with this young guy saying,
"Actually, don't come.

00:37:05.190 --> 00:37:06.240
This is difficult."

00:37:06.240 --> 00:37:08.680
Because again, the
narrative usually is like,

00:37:09.827 --> 00:37:12.237
"Come, it's easy." It's not like that.

00:37:12.237 --> 00:37:17.237
And actually when you work for a while

00:37:17.850 --> 00:37:20.610
on the subject of migration
in this part of the world,

00:37:20.610 --> 00:37:23.640
which I lived now for more than 10 years,

00:37:23.640 --> 00:37:26.286
you realize story after story,

00:37:26.286 --> 00:37:30.570
that a very small minority of these people

00:37:30.570 --> 00:37:32.970
really wanted to come to Europe.

00:37:32.970 --> 00:37:34.470
They've been forced to come to Europe.

00:37:34.470 --> 00:37:36.997
It's something to come out
many times in the documentary.

00:37:36.997 --> 00:37:40.984
But I had the possibility
to experience very well.

00:37:40.984 --> 00:37:45.984
It's you, Lucio was was
talking a bit of Arab Springs,

00:37:46.830 --> 00:37:49.310
which is of course the,
they say the starting of

00:37:50.355 --> 00:37:53.826
a very important historical point for

00:37:53.826 --> 00:37:56.430
all these migrations thing.

00:37:56.430 --> 00:38:00.167
And when the war started in Libya,

00:38:00.167 --> 00:38:04.230
there are millions of Africans
from everywhere in Africa,

00:38:04.230 --> 00:38:08.640
working in Libya that have
absolutely no interest

00:38:08.640 --> 00:38:09.663
in coming to Europe,

00:38:10.530 --> 00:38:14.160
that are working in Libya
and getting salaries

00:38:14.160 --> 00:38:17.250
that most of the times
are better than the ones

00:38:17.250 --> 00:38:21.090
that we get in Sicily and
they are not thinking at all

00:38:21.090 --> 00:38:23.130
to come to Europe.

00:38:23.130 --> 00:38:25.050
Of course, to make clear,

00:38:25.050 --> 00:38:29.523
I'm not defending Gaddafi,
but it is to say that-

00:38:30.364 --> 00:38:31.644
- That was the situation.

00:38:31.644 --> 00:38:32.975
- Yeah, exactly.

00:38:32.975 --> 00:38:34.976
- It is a fact.

00:38:34.976 --> 00:38:38.700
- A lot of mostly the people that we,

00:38:38.700 --> 00:38:42.630
that arrive in Europe and I
would say, I know a bit less,

00:38:42.630 --> 00:38:44.310
but I would say that
probably is the same in the

00:38:44.310 --> 00:38:45.840
United States.

00:38:45.840 --> 00:38:48.510
People arrive in the
United States will probably

00:38:48.510 --> 00:38:51.652
prefer to just stay at
home with the family

00:38:51.652 --> 00:38:55.440
in their country of origin.

00:38:55.440 --> 00:38:58.320
It just, it's not always possible.

00:38:58.320 --> 00:39:03.320
And this documentary shows
it from another side,

00:39:03.630 --> 00:39:06.355
a side to try to humanize
as much as possible,

00:39:06.355 --> 00:39:07.773
the human being.

00:39:14.244 --> 00:39:16.680
- [Speaker] I think that
I understood at least one,

00:39:16.680 --> 00:39:18.630
maybe two of the speakers,

00:39:18.630 --> 00:39:22.920
to express an opinion
that the European Union

00:39:22.920 --> 00:39:27.764
was not sufficient in its policies.

00:39:27.764 --> 00:39:31.762
Am I right that both of those speakers,

00:39:31.762 --> 00:39:34.200
or the one if there was only one,

00:39:34.200 --> 00:39:37.443
was in fact Maltese and not migrant?

00:39:39.240 --> 00:39:40.860
But it's an interesting question,

00:39:40.860 --> 00:39:45.860
what they think should be
better about European Union's

00:39:48.375 --> 00:39:52.283
policy in so far as it exists

00:39:52.283 --> 00:39:55.510
and whether what they expect to improve

00:39:56.430 --> 00:39:58.260
should legitimately be hoped for,

00:39:58.260 --> 00:40:01.080
or whether they're just dreaming

00:40:01.080 --> 00:40:02.880
about something that couldn't be better,

00:40:02.880 --> 00:40:07.470
that is realistically
not likely to happen.

00:40:07.470 --> 00:40:08.670
- I would say something about this

00:40:08.670 --> 00:40:10.110
and then I will to leave us all to Lucio,

00:40:10.110 --> 00:40:13.682
who has very important
experience about this.

00:40:13.682 --> 00:40:18.682
Actually one of the, I suppose
that you're talking about

00:40:18.840 --> 00:40:20.550
the woman that is speaking in Italian,

00:40:20.550 --> 00:40:25.550
she's the director of an NGO
that is rescuing people between

00:40:27.630 --> 00:40:29.400
in the Mediterranean.

00:40:29.400 --> 00:40:32.970
And talking about there are many things

00:40:32.970 --> 00:40:35.616
that European citizenship could do better,

00:40:35.616 --> 00:40:36.933
I will address one of them.

00:40:36.933 --> 00:40:39.987
I will leave to Lucio, that
has a lot of experience

00:40:39.987 --> 00:40:42.437
again about this, which
is the Dublin convention.

00:40:43.620 --> 00:40:47.910
Under this rule, which
is a European Union rule,

00:40:47.910 --> 00:40:51.540
when a human being, let's
try to use this word,

00:40:51.540 --> 00:40:56.540
when a human being reach
Europe is allowed to

00:40:57.138 --> 00:41:01.470
ask for asylum just in
the country of arrival

00:41:01.470 --> 00:41:02.970
and not in another country.

00:41:02.970 --> 00:41:05.220
So, if a migrant arrived in Malta

00:41:05.220 --> 00:41:10.220
can ask for a political
asylum just in Malta,

00:41:10.560 --> 00:41:12.806
cannot travel to Italy or
to Germany or to France

00:41:12.806 --> 00:41:17.220
or either cannot ask for
asylum in France from Malta

00:41:17.220 --> 00:41:20.163
to France from Malta, can
just do that in Malta.

00:41:21.155 --> 00:41:26.155
This is a huge problem for
many reasons, first of all.

00:41:26.453 --> 00:41:29.097
Because of course, the
southern countries of Europe

00:41:29.097 --> 00:41:34.097
have this problem, actually
this amount of responsibility

00:41:34.560 --> 00:41:37.290
in comparison with the
northern countries of Europe.

00:41:37.290 --> 00:41:40.830
But also because, mostly of course,

00:41:40.830 --> 00:41:42.560
the human beings arriving
don't want to stay

00:41:42.560 --> 00:41:44.968
in the south part of
Europe and would like to go

00:41:44.968 --> 00:41:47.226
in the northern part of Europe.

00:41:47.226 --> 00:41:52.226
And also because in the
specific case of Malta,

00:41:52.230 --> 00:41:55.080
you can can probably
imagine that very easily.

00:41:55.080 --> 00:41:58.140
Most of the human beings
arriving in Malta,

00:41:58.140 --> 00:41:59.983
they don't know what Malta is.

00:41:59.983 --> 00:42:04.200
Of course, they took a
boat from the North Africa,

00:42:04.200 --> 00:42:07.590
thinking they would reach
Europe and they end in Malta,

00:42:07.590 --> 00:42:09.810
which is a very small island

00:42:09.810 --> 00:42:11.520
in the middle of the Mediterranean.

00:42:11.520 --> 00:42:13.170
They didn't know it existed,

00:42:13.170 --> 00:42:15.330
they didn't know it's a country.

00:42:15.330 --> 00:42:16.920
It's- can you imagine?

00:42:16.920 --> 00:42:19.410
They are there and they're stuck there.

00:42:19.410 --> 00:42:21.990
They basically can't move from there.

00:42:21.990 --> 00:42:23.490
They can't go anywhere else.

00:42:23.490 --> 00:42:26.970
They can either get an
asylum, political asylum there

00:42:26.970 --> 00:42:31.860
or move back, which add
another layer of problems

00:42:31.860 --> 00:42:32.910
to what they say.

00:42:32.910 --> 00:42:34.458
And I will leave it to Lucio, who

00:42:34.458 --> 00:42:37.158
definitely has something
to say about this.

00:42:37.158 --> 00:42:41.910
- Maybe I will try to
be systematic but short,

00:42:41.910 --> 00:42:45.213
because this is a huge question
you are raising, actually.

00:42:47.340 --> 00:42:50.940
First, you said in so
far as there is a policy,

00:42:50.940 --> 00:42:53.010
don't get me, don't be mistaken.

00:42:53.010 --> 00:42:54.750
There is a policy.

00:42:54.750 --> 00:42:59.750
Europe, the European Union has
deployed a significant force

00:43:03.382 --> 00:43:05.474
of protection of the
border called Frontex,

00:43:05.474 --> 00:43:07.560
which is very, very powerful.

00:43:07.560 --> 00:43:09.750
It is mentioned in the documentary.

00:43:09.750 --> 00:43:11.790
Frontex what is Frontex?

00:43:11.790 --> 00:43:16.790
Frontex is a quasi-federalized agency

00:43:18.870 --> 00:43:22.590
that complements the control
of the external voters

00:43:22.590 --> 00:43:26.460
of the Union in the areas that
have particular difficulties

00:43:26.460 --> 00:43:28.710
that are now widespread.

00:43:28.710 --> 00:43:32.160
Because with the recent Belarus crisis,

00:43:32.160 --> 00:43:33.660
then we have it in the Baltics,

00:43:33.660 --> 00:43:38.100
we have them down in
Bulgaria versus Turkey.

00:43:38.100 --> 00:43:39.510
We have them in Greece,

00:43:39.510 --> 00:43:41.490
we have them in Italy and Malta,

00:43:41.490 --> 00:43:43.284
and now also in the Canary Islands,

00:43:43.284 --> 00:43:46.233
because of the crossings from Morocco.

00:43:47.220 --> 00:43:50.130
So you see that this is now spread around,

00:43:50.130 --> 00:43:54.930
we are talking about numbers
that are becoming serious.

00:43:54.930 --> 00:43:59.930
Over 10,000 men and women
permanently employed

00:44:00.510 --> 00:44:04.890
and they have a couple of
billions of viewers available,

00:44:04.890 --> 00:44:07.710
which is equal to a couple
of billions of dollars.

00:44:07.710 --> 00:44:10.890
So although it's not huge numbers,

00:44:10.890 --> 00:44:12.603
it becomes a serious number.

00:44:13.650 --> 00:44:15.780
And that makes a difference.

00:44:15.780 --> 00:44:17.790
They are patrolling,

00:44:17.790 --> 00:44:20.664
they dispose of the best
equipment in terms of

00:44:20.664 --> 00:44:23.013
satellite control.

00:44:23.910 --> 00:44:26.970
They use the same kind of
satellite systems that the

00:44:26.970 --> 00:44:30.990
military and the Navy of
NATO uses in Mediterranean.

00:44:30.990 --> 00:44:34.020
They're sharing the
same kind of equipment.

00:44:34.020 --> 00:44:36.690
So they are extremely sophisticated,

00:44:36.690 --> 00:44:39.423
close to what you see with
the homeland securities

00:44:39.423 --> 00:44:41.553
that has been developed here.

00:44:43.020 --> 00:44:44.193
And that's a policy.

00:44:45.150 --> 00:44:48.330
So, that was not existent 10 years ago.

00:44:48.330 --> 00:44:53.330
The sacrosanct protection of
the frontures of your country

00:44:54.150 --> 00:44:56.820
that was considered by
the patriots to be in

00:44:56.820 --> 00:44:58.830
for the countries, you
know, European Union,

00:44:58.830 --> 00:44:59.703
nothing to do.

00:45:00.600 --> 00:45:05.600
And no, all the extremists
or less extremists of Europe

00:45:06.150 --> 00:45:09.930
are now behind the existence of a federal

00:45:09.930 --> 00:45:13.830
European Union policy
concerned with Frontex.

00:45:13.830 --> 00:45:18.300
But when it comes to a
federal, quasi-federal approach

00:45:18.300 --> 00:45:21.660
to the influx of these human beings

00:45:21.660 --> 00:45:26.040
that is generically labeled as migrants,

00:45:26.040 --> 00:45:28.620
then there it disappears.

00:45:28.620 --> 00:45:29.453
Why?

00:45:29.453 --> 00:45:32.350
Because the main drive there

00:45:32.350 --> 00:45:35.043
is what you have heard
in this documentary,

00:45:37.444 --> 00:45:40.800
protection of your country means
protection of your culture,

00:45:40.800 --> 00:45:45.800
protection of your customs,
protection of your religion.

00:45:46.860 --> 00:45:50.910
Hence, each states keeps it to itself.

00:45:50.910 --> 00:45:51.843
So the Dublin,

00:45:57.150 --> 00:45:59.430
the Dublin treaty or Dublin agreement,

00:45:59.430 --> 00:46:03.210
which is now translated into
European Union regulations,

00:46:03.210 --> 00:46:06.090
it's actually very progressive in terms of

00:46:06.090 --> 00:46:09.300
asylum-seeking rights.

00:46:09.300 --> 00:46:13.560
There is a wealth of legislation

00:46:13.560 --> 00:46:16.410
that is actually very progressive.

00:46:16.410 --> 00:46:20.460
And I can state without
exaggerating that it is

00:46:20.460 --> 00:46:23.070
one of the most progressive in the world.

00:46:23.070 --> 00:46:27.000
The problem is the limitation
that Mauro is indicating.

00:46:27.000 --> 00:46:32.000
So, what is lacking is
exactly that, crossing.

00:46:32.018 --> 00:46:37.018
We like having a federalized
police at the border.

00:46:37.170 --> 00:46:41.880
We don't like having a federalized policy

00:46:41.880 --> 00:46:44.040
of distributing the people,

00:46:44.040 --> 00:46:46.540
because the problem is not with the

00:46:48.510 --> 00:46:51.270
moment when you disembark.

00:46:51.270 --> 00:46:55.110
Somebody should know if that
person embarking in Malta,

00:46:55.110 --> 00:46:58.870
it's Malta taking care
of the identification

00:47:00.030 --> 00:47:04.020
and providing or not the
status of refugee, for example.

00:47:04.020 --> 00:47:05.550
All right?

00:47:05.550 --> 00:47:09.330
That could be perfectly
okay, provided that Malta

00:47:09.330 --> 00:47:12.543
is not stuck with all these
people that do not go anywhere.

00:47:14.460 --> 00:47:16.353
So, the redistribution.

00:47:17.370 --> 00:47:22.370
And redistribution hits the
wall of this kind of discourse

00:47:22.860 --> 00:47:24.933
that you have heard in the documentary.

00:47:26.250 --> 00:47:30.060
Yes, and I unfortunately have
to say is the same debate

00:47:30.060 --> 00:47:33.330
that you have here in the
United States, identical.

00:47:33.330 --> 00:47:35.040
I can mention one person,

00:47:35.040 --> 00:47:38.430
although I don't know if this
is caution enough or not,

00:47:38.430 --> 00:47:41.790
but if I mention Mr. Bannon, Steve Bannon,

00:47:41.790 --> 00:47:46.107
you may have heard of him,
former aide of Mr. Trump.

00:47:46.107 --> 00:47:51.107
He was pursuing the same
policies in Europe throughout.

00:47:51.960 --> 00:47:55.200
He is called as a guru by certain quarters

00:47:55.200 --> 00:47:56.924
in European politics.

00:47:56.924 --> 00:48:00.930
There is a clear direct
connection even through the

00:48:00.930 --> 00:48:04.410
same persons on the two
sides of the Atlantic.

00:48:04.410 --> 00:48:07.770
So, we are confronted to a generational,

00:48:07.770 --> 00:48:12.770
cultural and therefore,
political issue here.

00:48:13.242 --> 00:48:15.450
And sorry to come back
to my starting point

00:48:15.450 --> 00:48:17.043
of this exchange,

00:48:17.043 --> 00:48:20.913
human dignity and human
rights are individual.

00:48:22.290 --> 00:48:27.290
We cannot dispose of groups of
people undefined as migrants

00:48:28.980 --> 00:48:33.980
and keep them like parcels
in a warehouse somewhere

00:48:35.010 --> 00:48:36.952
in one country or the other.

00:48:36.952 --> 00:48:39.843
And that we need to solve.

00:48:40.710 --> 00:48:44.253
We must solve it. It's unsustainable.

00:48:55.620 --> 00:48:56.490
- [Speaker] Can I follow up?

00:48:56.490 --> 00:49:01.490
So what happens when they are in Malta?

00:49:01.890 --> 00:49:04.440
So, we're talking about
like the government

00:49:04.440 --> 00:49:09.440
stuck with people not there
and that, so I just, this is-

00:49:11.121 --> 00:49:12.471
- What happens?

00:49:12.471 --> 00:49:14.284
- [Speaker] What happened
after that, people are-

00:49:14.284 --> 00:49:16.230
- Yeah, but what happens before also?

00:49:16.230 --> 00:49:18.840
Because if you are in Malta

00:49:18.840 --> 00:49:20.790
and you're stuck with all these people,

00:49:22.560 --> 00:49:24.900
the question that you are
asking is what happens next?

00:49:24.900 --> 00:49:29.550
If I am an asylum seeker
and I am given asylum status

00:49:29.550 --> 00:49:33.727
because in my country, I cannot go back.

00:49:33.727 --> 00:49:35.913
[Indistinct] and Ethiopia,

00:49:38.060 --> 00:49:39.480
I mean, Sudan are clear examples.

00:49:39.480 --> 00:49:41.580
You cannot push these people back.

00:49:41.580 --> 00:49:43.140
Not only it's inhuman,

00:49:43.140 --> 00:49:45.570
it's in violation of existing convention,

00:49:45.570 --> 00:49:49.181
international conventions
like the refugee convention in

00:49:49.181 --> 00:49:50.464
1951 of the UI, no?

00:49:50.464 --> 00:49:52.290
So, we cannot do.

00:49:52.290 --> 00:49:54.420
So, Malta is stuck with these people,

00:49:54.420 --> 00:49:55.743
they must keep them.

00:49:56.790 --> 00:49:59.193
So, what is the alternative for Malta?

00:50:01.680 --> 00:50:05.430
Push back as many as possible because

00:50:05.430 --> 00:50:09.813
we heard that as again,
we cannot get more,

00:50:11.100 --> 00:50:14.190
which is not entirely correct,

00:50:14.190 --> 00:50:17.880
because (laughs) this is a perception, no?

00:50:17.880 --> 00:50:18.900
We are full.

00:50:18.900 --> 00:50:20.783
Full of what? (laughs)

00:50:20.783 --> 00:50:23.280
There is full of request of people working

00:50:23.280 --> 00:50:25.950
and doing jobs that nobody takes in Malta

00:50:25.950 --> 00:50:27.963
as much as in Italy and elsewhere.

00:50:28.860 --> 00:50:33.860
But this idea that if we open
up the working opportunities.

00:50:35.160 --> 00:50:38.100
Here I come with the word,

00:50:38.100 --> 00:50:40.470
then you open up to a pull factor.

00:50:40.470 --> 00:50:43.020
This is the word in politics in Europe

00:50:43.020 --> 00:50:45.963
and also in this state, in this country.

00:50:47.190 --> 00:50:51.360
Pull factor meaning you
actually incite people to come

00:50:51.360 --> 00:50:53.280
because they will find work.

00:50:53.280 --> 00:50:55.023
So, you don't give them anything.

00:50:56.100 --> 00:51:00.360
So that it shows
exemplarily that you will,

00:51:00.360 --> 00:51:02.610
it's useless to come.

00:51:02.610 --> 00:51:05.340
So, what happens is before and after.

00:51:05.340 --> 00:51:08.520
After, you keep them completely separated

00:51:08.520 --> 00:51:11.370
to make it an example for the outside.

00:51:11.370 --> 00:51:15.090
And before, you make it sure that you

00:51:15.090 --> 00:51:16.620
take them the least possible,

00:51:16.620 --> 00:51:20.490
including you, one creates
problems to the NGOs

00:51:20.490 --> 00:51:22.143
that save people at sea.

00:51:23.190 --> 00:51:27.480
This case I was involved
in had this judgment.

00:51:27.480 --> 00:51:30.750
The first topic was exactly about that,

00:51:30.750 --> 00:51:34.530
Italy putting all possible
obstacles to the NGOs

00:51:34.530 --> 00:51:36.993
that save people at sea, why?

00:51:38.285 --> 00:51:41.610
Because by doing that the
pull factor is diminished.

00:51:41.610 --> 00:51:44.730
People should know that if they
try to cross, they will die.

00:51:44.730 --> 00:51:45.783
So they don't come.

00:51:48.540 --> 00:51:53.190
Nothing of that, neither before nor after,

00:51:53.190 --> 00:51:56.583
seems to me in line with the principles.

00:51:58.680 --> 00:52:00.150
They are not.

00:52:00.150 --> 00:52:03.423
I think it is objective, it
is not a simple perception.

00:52:04.440 --> 00:52:05.430
- Absolutely, and also,

00:52:05.430 --> 00:52:08.310
to add something which is very better

00:52:08.310 --> 00:52:12.000
but I think again we come
back to the same image.

00:52:12.000 --> 00:52:15.990
It's important to insist.

00:52:15.990 --> 00:52:18.390
You asked what happened when they,

00:52:18.390 --> 00:52:20.100
after they arrived.

00:52:20.100 --> 00:52:25.100
Basically one thing which
is one more time absurd.

00:52:27.007 --> 00:52:30.510
There is an officer at some point deciding

00:52:30.510 --> 00:52:33.840
if this human being traveled to Malta

00:52:33.840 --> 00:52:38.190
as an economical migrant
or an asylum-seeker.

00:52:38.190 --> 00:52:41.940
So, there is somebody from
an office deciding this.

00:52:41.940 --> 00:52:43.620
There would be an interview

00:52:43.620 --> 00:52:46.080
and they will even
check if you have scars,

00:52:46.080 --> 00:52:51.080
if you have a psychological
situation that might be related

00:52:51.780 --> 00:52:54.902
to some kind violence you suffered

00:52:54.902 --> 00:52:56.940
in your country of origin.

00:52:56.940 --> 00:52:58.410
And then after this interview,

00:52:58.410 --> 00:53:02.700
and after the review of
your psychological case,

00:53:02.700 --> 00:53:05.100
your physical case, the
country you're coming,

00:53:05.100 --> 00:53:07.830
they will decide, okay,
you are traveling here

00:53:07.830 --> 00:53:10.470
because there is a problem in your country

00:53:10.470 --> 00:53:12.698
or you are traveling here just because

00:53:12.698 --> 00:53:14.463
you want a better future.

00:53:15.330 --> 00:53:19.860
The second case, you are
denied asylum of course.

00:53:19.860 --> 00:53:24.513
Because to want a be future
is not enough to come here.

00:53:25.381 --> 00:53:30.381
In the first case, if you
clearly show signs of problems

00:53:31.339 --> 00:53:33.330
from your country of origin,

00:53:33.330 --> 00:53:38.330
then you might be allowed to
stay with a status of refugee,

00:53:40.230 --> 00:53:44.730
which then leads again
to all the complications

00:53:44.730 --> 00:53:47.101
because when you got your refugee status,

00:53:47.101 --> 00:53:50.779
this also important, but
this would be a very long

00:53:50.779 --> 00:53:53.947
discussion, because sometimes
we had the sensation that

00:53:53.947 --> 00:53:55.800
"Okay, you got your refugee status,

00:53:55.800 --> 00:53:59.670
now your life is paradise"
is not exactly right.

00:53:59.670 --> 00:54:01.110
Also, the refugee status actually,

00:54:01.110 --> 00:54:05.880
that is not guaranteeing a
very necessarily happy life.

00:54:05.880 --> 00:54:07.890
It's good, it's a good way to start

00:54:07.890 --> 00:54:10.320
unless you have the
right to stay in Europe.

00:54:10.320 --> 00:54:11.790
But still there are a lot of limitation

00:54:11.790 --> 00:54:16.790
and a lot of criteria that
you continuously need to

00:54:18.630 --> 00:54:19.953
reaffirm to stay.

00:54:24.390 --> 00:54:28.081
- [Speaker] Okay. So, thank
you very much to both of you.

00:54:28.081 --> 00:54:31.397
Lucio Gussetti, visiting fellow

00:54:31.397 --> 00:54:34.290
at the European Studies Council

00:54:34.290 --> 00:54:39.290
and to Mauro Mondello,
for your discussions.

00:54:41.430 --> 00:54:44.640
Very fruitful discussions
that we had on the topic.

00:54:44.640 --> 00:54:48.780
And I hope then to be
able to have you back

00:54:48.780 --> 00:54:51.459
at another opportunity with your thoughts.

00:54:51.459 --> 00:54:52.658
- Thank you.
- Thank you.

00:54:52.658 --> 00:54:53.874
- Thank you very much.
- Thank you.

00:54:53.874 --> 00:54:56.124
(applause)

